      "rev_n_4_e13767_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_e13767_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_e13767",
        "thought_text": "Analyze the parity of A and B directly: the product A*B*C is odd if and only if A, B, and C are all odd. Since C can be chosen from {1, 2, 3}, we just need to check if there is an odd number in that set (which is always true: 1 and 3 are odd) and if A and B are both odd. If A and B are odd, pick C=1 (or 3) to make the product odd. If either A or B is even, no C will work.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8274041618547537182",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794506900.567404
      },
      "rev_n_5_ca6e3d_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_ca6e3d_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_ca6e3d",
        "thought_text": "Iterate through all three possible values of C (1, 2, 3). For each C, compute the product A*B*C and check if the result is odd using modulo 2. If any iteration yields an odd product, output Yes immediately. If the loop finishes without finding such a C, output No.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-8274041618547537182",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794506900.5674217
      },
      "rev_n_6_31cc11_0": {
